WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
Welcome back to SpaceX Daily, episode 44.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:04.700
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.900
Today is July 25th, 2026.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.780
Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.700
Starship Flight 13's V3 Starlink deployment

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.040
and intact splashdown clears the path

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.560
for a tower catch attempt on the next flight.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.260
SpaceX launched the 13th Starship Test flight

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.600
from Starbase, Texas at 5.51 PM Eastern Daylight time.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.240
The 397-foot tall, two-stage vehicle lifted off

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.880
under 33 methane-fueled Raptor engines

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.780
producing roughly 16 million pounds of thrust.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.240
The upper stage deployed 20 next-generation

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.080
Starlink V3 satellites during the mission.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.020
The stage then splashed down intact in the Indian Ocean

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.660
after completing its objectives,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.300
though the landing profile was imperfect.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.260
This flight marks the first Starship Test

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.880
since the June 2026 IPO and continues the ongoing campaign

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.680
that last appeared on Air in EP 43 on July 24,

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.980
moving the orbital refueling and reuse milestones forward

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.240
by demonstrating payload delivery

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.080
under near-full propellant loads.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.000
The July 25 Test marks Starship's first flight

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.220
after the company's NASDAQ listing on June 12th, 2026.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.220
The mission focused on satellite deployment

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.540
and recovery data collection under the post-IPO

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:27.940
operational spotlight.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.760
This flight supplies the first public performance data point

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.040
since the largest IPO in history

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:39.320
raised roughly $75 billion at a $1.8 trillion valuation.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.540
The outcome feeds directly into the reuse

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.260
and refueling timelines that determine future launch economics.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.280
Source reports from Google News note the post-listing timing

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.020
as a key marker for public performance tracking.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.040
The event also supplies early post-IPO data

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.120
on how the vehicle handles near-full propellant loads

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.900
during a scent and separation.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.600
The 13th flight carried and released the first batch

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.560
of next-generation Starlink V3 satellites.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.600
Deployment occurred despite the vehicle's

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.420
non-nominal landing profile.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.340
The satellites represent an upgrade path

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.920
that directly supports the constellation growth

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.960
and direct-to-cell open questions

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.700
tracked for the Starlink program.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.440
Early reports indicate the deployment sequence

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.160
completed its primary objectives

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.200
before the upper stage re-entered.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.460
Google News coverage highlighted the V3 batch

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.800
as the initial on-orbit demonstration

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.180
of the upgraded hardware.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.560
Community observers on R-slash SpaceX

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.720
linked the successful release to potential schedule impacts

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.840
on direct-to-cell service expansion.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.620
The 397-foot tall two-stage rocket lifted off

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.420
under the power of its 33 Raptor engines.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.480
The flight advanced Starship's test campaign

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.860
toward full reuse goals by demonstrating control descent

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.320
and stage separation.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.580
Reports describe the overall test as mostly successful

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.200
even with the noted landing imperfection on the upper stage.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.360
The booster's performance adds to the data set

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.680
on engine reliability and propellant management at scale.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.560
Google News summaries emphasized the super-heavy contribution

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.880
to reaching the velocity required for orbital insertion.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.640
The booster segment also supplied additional telemetry

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.120
on methane-fueled engine cluster behavior

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.880
during the initial ascent phase.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.180
SpaceX confirmed splash down of the Starship upper stage

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.220
following the 13th test flight.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.120
The vehicle completed its primary objectives

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.860
of satellite deployment despite an imperfect landing profile.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.800
The confirmation came via official channels

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.000
and was highlighted in community threads

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.200
as a positive data point for future recovery hardware.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.180
This outcome supplies additional flight data

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.900
on heat shield and attitude control performance

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.840
that the program needs before attempting tower catches.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.240
Reddit threads on R-slash SpaceX noted the intact ocean impact

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.680
as evidence that current recovery systems

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.780
can tolerate non-nominal conditions.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.820
The splash down data set now includes real world measurements

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.840
of thermal loads during reentry.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.340
With the flight data now in hand,

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.940
the next obvious question is how quickly SpaceX will close

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:24.740
the remaining gap to full reuse.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:29.360
Elon Musk posted on X that SpaceX will attempt to catch the ship

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.240
with the tower on the next flight

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.440
unless post-mission data review reveals problems.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.020
The statement came after the 13th test flight

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.520
successfully deployed satellites and confirmed splash down.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:45.260
The comment directly addresses the central open question

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.540
of full and rapid reuse that the program has tracked

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.100
since the prior episode.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:54.100
Observers noted the announcement as the clearest public signal

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.160
yet on the timeline for the first tower catch attempt.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.360
While the Starship team reviews that landing data,

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.520
another part of the Musk ecosystem moved at its own rapid pace.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.840
XAI released GROC 4.6 and 4.7 back-to-back,

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.740
establishing a release cadence unmatched

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:14.100
by other frontier labs.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.540
The updates continue iterative model improvements

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:20.320
running on the company's expanding compute clusters.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.840
This development sits within the broader Musk compute ecosystem

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.320
that now includes SpaceX and Starlink infrastructure

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.880
for potential backhaul and orbital data center concepts.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.740
No specific cluster size or training run details

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.400
were disclosed in the reports.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:40.900
Meanwhile, the company's more mature launch cadence

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:42.340
continues on the West Coast.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.520
SpaceX is preparing a Falcon 9 launch

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.700
from Vandenberg Space Force Base for Saturday morning.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.280
The mission continues the company's high cadence operations

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.660
on the West Coast pad, where booster reuse counts keep climbing.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.980
This launch sits outside the Starship Campaign

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.820
and helps sustain the annual launch rate ceiling

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.080
that remains a key open question for the Falcon program.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.940
No payload details were released in the targeting notice.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:14.160
Google News targeting notices place the Saturday morning window

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.620
as the next scheduled West Coast activity.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.000
The mission supports ongoing booster flight counts

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.700
that continue to rise with each reuse cycle.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.640
Observers on our SpaceX highlighted the rapid turnaround

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.760
from the prior test flight to this successful deployment.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.920
Some noted the vehicle's survival in the ocean

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.160
as a positive data point for future recovery hardware.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.140
Community threads discussed how the V3 satellites performance

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.020
could affect direct to cell timelines.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:45.960
Multiple users flagged the Musk Tower catch statement

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.800
as the clearest signal yet on reuse progress.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.500
Others compared this flight's timeline

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.880
to earlier Starship tests that ended in loss of vehicle.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.500
The discussion threads also examined

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.360
how the intact splashdown data might influence

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.240
guidance algorithm updates ahead of the next mission.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:06.580
Worth keeping an eye on several reports describe

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:10.480
the landing as imperfect even though splashdown was confirmed.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.320
An imperfect profile leaves open questions about thermal

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.000
protection and control margins that must be closed

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.740
before tower catch attempts.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:22.720
Resolving the issue would require post flight data review

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.040
of guidance, navigation, and heat shield performance.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.300
Counterpoint coverage in multiple outlets

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.980
stressed that these margins must be quantified

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.140
before any tower catch hardware is wrist on a cent.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:38.740
The review process will examine entry guidance logs

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.060
and tile performance metrics collected

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.940
during the non-nominal descent.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:48.760
On the AI front, XAI released GROC 4.6 and 4.7

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.180
in immediate succession, establishing a release pace

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.200
not yet matched by peer labs.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:57.900
The updates continue the company's push to iterate models

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.860
on its expanding compute clusters.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:03.320
This activity occurs within the Musk ecosystem

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:07.180
that increasingly links XAI training runs to SpaceX

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:10.180
and Starlink infrastructure for potential backhaul

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:12.040
and orbital compute concepts.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.020
No specific details on cluster scale

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.260
or cross-company data sharing agreements

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.300
were disclosed in the available reports.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.160
Google News aggregations framed the back-to-back releases

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.340
as a distinct operational tempo

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:27.280
within the frontier model space.

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:29.520
From an engineering standpoint,

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:32.740
the 13th flight demonstrated Starship's ability

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:35.160
to carry and release a meaningful payload

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.780
while surviving re-entry and ocean impact.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:41.580
At the core of this result is the two-stage architecture.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.780
The super heavy boosters 33 Raptor engines

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:47.580
provide the initial 16 million pounds of thrust

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:50.560
needed to reach the velocity for orbital insertion,

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.540
after which the upper-stage's own Raptors

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:56.880
handle the final circularization and de-orbit burns.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.820
The intact splash-down shows that the current heat shield

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:03.300
tile layout and attitude control margins

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.940
can keep the vehicle within survivable thermal

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.660
and structural loads even when the landing burn

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:10.320
is not perfectly executed.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.600
That margin matters because every extra kilogram

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.780
of propellant or structure saved on the upper-stage

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:19.540
directly increases the payload that can be delivered

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:22.100
to orbit or retained for a tower catch.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:25.820
The design choice to fly a near-full propellant load

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.220
on this test also validates the tank

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:30.420
and feed system performance at scale,

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.900
reducing uncertainty around the orbital refueling

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.620
demonstrations required for deeper space missions.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:39.880
The flight therefore supplies concrete data

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:43.000
on how the current thermal protection and guidance stack

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:45.500
behaves under real reentry conditions,

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:49.400
rather than relying solely on ground test extrapolations.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.780
Next flights will likely tighten the landing cord

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.280
or by refining the entry guidance algorithms

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:58.700
and possibly adjusting tile density or gap fillers.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.480
If those changes close the remaining gap,

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:04.140
the program can shift from proving survival

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:07.100
to proving rapid reuse, which is the step

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:09.720
that collapses the cost per kilogram to orbit.

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:12.480
The idiot index here is instructive.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.900
The raw materials and propellant inside a starship

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:18.920
upper-stage cost far less than the finished vehicle,

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:22.420
yet each successful recovery reduces the multiplier

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:24.800
by amortizing the high-value hardware

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.680
across multiple missions instead of discarding it

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:28.800
after one use.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.180
Closing the landing precision gap,

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:33.580
therefore directly attacks that multiplier

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.260
and moves the economics of orbital delivery

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:39.320
closer to the theoretical floor set by propellant

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:40.640
and metal prices alone.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:45.640
And a quick market note, SPCX closed at $115.07.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:50.440
Before we go, Starship's flight data now feeds

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.940
directly into the next reuse milestone.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:56.600
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

